WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.421 --> 00:00:02.730
- So, your son is now four.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:06.510
And I hear he corrects you
when you're speaking Spanish.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:07.343
Is this true?
- Oh, yeah.

00:00:07.343 --> 00:00:08.337
Yeah.
- He's four.

00:00:08.337 --> 00:00:10.057
- Yeah, he's four.
- Okay.

00:00:10.057 --> 00:00:12.660
- 'Cause his first language is Spanish.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.190
I didn't grow up speaking Spanish.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.360
But because we live in Mexico City,

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.090
I was just shooting in
Spain, he's super fluent.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.190
And he corrects me all the time.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.870
And he does it with attitude.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.693
- He's like, disgusted-
- He's, "Oh my god, mom."

00:00:23.693 --> 00:00:26.220
And I'm like, "What? You're four."

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.300
Easy, easy.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:28.260
- I'm disappointed.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.590
- Yeah, he's so
disappointed in my Spanish.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:31.440
He is.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.273
- It's way easier.

00:00:32.273 --> 00:00:33.840
It's so cool that he is
learning at such a young age.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.800
It's easier.
- Yeah.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.450
- You know, dialect, all of
that is easier to pick up then.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.670
So he sounds very wise for his age.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.070
- He's super smart.
- Okay.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.423
- He's brilliant.

00:00:42.423 --> 00:00:44.700
- He's brilliant, if I say so myself.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.740
- So both of our mamas were teachers.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.510
Were you a good student?

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:49.800
- I was a great student.
- I feel like you have to be.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:50.997
- But I felt like I had to be.

00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:53.670
And my mom, she was like...

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.050
In the moment, she was so annoying

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.700
'cause she would go to
the school for everything.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:57.757
And she would go and ask my teacher,

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:58.590
"Why does she get a B?

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:00.367
I read this, feels like an A."

00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:01.800
"My daughter needs to be in math class

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:02.940
and she needs to be on this track."

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.220
And so to have a mom like that

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.160
advocate for you at school
is super life changing

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.660
and important, looking back now.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.303
So God bless all the
teachers in the world.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.410
So I ended up being a great
student because she made me.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:17.243
- That's amazing.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:18.720
- Were you a good student?
- My mom's so angry

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.738
right now at home.

00:01:19.738 --> 00:01:21.030
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:21.870
- I was not a...

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.480
I think the only thing that
kept me in school was the arts.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:25.350
Like I just...

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.150
I love learning.

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.290
I'm an avid learner.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:29.340
I love reading, I love all that.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.750
I just didn't like...

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.210
I just think, and
especially having kids now,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.040
people like to learn differently.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.660
Like, you know, we learn differently.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:40.560
And it's very hard for me
to sit still in a desk and-

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.680
- But the fact that we still
learn that way is weird.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:44.513
- I know, it's just...

00:01:44.513 --> 00:01:46.080
It was just very hard for me

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.469
for that many hours in a day to be like,

00:01:48.469 --> 00:01:50.627
worksheet, worksheet.
- It's hard for most kids.

00:01:50.627 --> 00:01:51.660
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Still.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.430
And especially like,
technology has disrupted

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:54.780
so many industries.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.870
Every industry, healthcare,
how we buy stuff.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.465
I mean, everything, but yet
it hasn't quite disrupted

00:02:00.465 --> 00:02:03.030
how we teach in the classroom.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:03.870
It should have.

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:04.800
It should.
- Which is interesting

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.750
'cause you thought COVID
would've changed that a tiny bit

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:07.590
just 'cause we all were on Zoom.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.713
But anyway, glad that's
not still happening.

